Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how to use the **`NodeIdConverter.format`** helper to append the node‑ID (if it exists) to a `StringBuilder` that you’re building up for a log entry.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.logging.log4j.LogEvent;   // or whatever LogEvent type you’re using
import java.lang.StringBuilder;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.  Create the objects you’ll be working with
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
StringBuilder logBuilder = new StringBuilder();          // the buffer you’re building
LogEvent logEvent = /* obtain your LogEvent instance here */;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.  Call the helper – it will append the node‑ID if it can find one
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
functions.NodeIdConverter_format(
        new functions.NodeIdConverter_format.Params() {{
            event      = logEvent;   // the LogEvent that may contain the node‑ID
            toAppendTo = logBuilder; // the StringBuilder to which the ID will be appended
        }}
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3.  Inspect the result
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
System.out.println("Log entry so far: " + logBuilder.toString());
```

### What the helper does

* **`event`** – The `LogEvent` you’re processing.  
  The helper looks inside this event for the node‑ID (typically stored in the event’s context or metadata).  
* **`toAppendTo`** – The `StringBuilder` you want to extend.  
  If the node‑ID is present, the helper appends it *directly* to this builder; otherwise it leaves the builder unchanged.

### Why this works

* The helper is written in Java 8 syntax, so you can call it directly from any Java 8+ code.  
* It accepts the objects themselves (`LogEvent` and `StringBuilder`) rather than their string representations, so you don’t need to serialize them first.  
* The helper is idempotent: calling it multiple times on the same builder will simply keep appending the node‑ID each time (or nothing if the ID isn’t there).

### Quick sanity check

If you want to verify that the node‑ID was actually appended, you can add a simple check after the call:

```java
if (logBuilder.indexOf("nodeId=") >= 0) {
    System.out.println("Node ID was appended.");
} else {
    System.out.println("No node ID found in the LogEvent.");
}
```

That’s all you need—just plug the helper into your logging pipeline and the node‑ID will be added automatically whenever it’s available.

